  "name": "Question 9",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14134102/assets/cbf6838fbb72a7fcffbb621b813abbb5d1ccd0a517fcbba7878d4be3f64e8c11.png)\n\nGiven the picture shown above, which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $M,$ $R,$ and $N$ are collinear\n\nII. $T,$ $S,$ and $P$ are collinear\n\nIII. $M,$ $R,$ and $S$ are **not** collinear",
  "worked_solution": "Three (or more) points are collinear if a straight line passes through all points.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our diagrams in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. There is a straight line that passes through these three points.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14134102/assets/bf251981278e739aaefe7c62cfa8f50d4be39ffa079e358824aaeec2b1045469.png)\n\n \n\n- Statement II is false. We can't draw a straight line that passes through these three points.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14134102/assets/aff25fea8765f1abf339eb406c1fe629699ede85a0afabd828e04c8a87449879.png)\n\n- Statement III is true. We can't draw a straight line that passes through these three points.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14134102/assets/28e81082c0f342992b9123c88c39d288a53e9b30dc4f39ee048739349cd38425.png)\n\n \n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"",
